{
  "id": "drivetrain",
  "name": "Drivetrain",
  "summary": "Front-mid LT V8 drives through a flywheel and clutch into a TREMEC TR-6060 six-speed (ZL1 1LE RPO MH3, 650 lb-ft rated), a two-piece propeller shaft and a cradle-mounted 226 mm differential with GM eLSD (3.73:1) feeding independent rear halfshafts. Race builds typically fit a twin/multi-disc clutch with an adjustable HRB in an SFI bellhousing, a stronger H-pattern or sequential gearbox, a one-piece carbon driveshaft with safety loops, a mechanical race LSD with solid diff mounts, upgraded halfshafts, and ducted trans/diff oil coolers.",
  "overview": "Layout. On the Gen 6 (Alpha platform) Camaro the engine sits behind the front axle with the bellhousing face at about x=0.38 m. The TREMEC 6060 (TR-6060) is a fully synchronized aluminum-case six-speed with triple-cone 1st/2nd and double-cone 3rd-6th/reverse synchronizers, tapered-roller main and countershaft bearings, solenoid 2-3 skip-shift and reverse inhibit, and a remote shifter linked by an external shift rod. GM rates it at 650 lb-ft (881 N\u00b7m). The ZL1 1LE close-ratio MH3 box runs 2.29/1.61/1.21/1.00/0.82/0.68 (R 3.11) with a 3.73 final drive; 2016+ SS uses 2.66/1.78/1.30/1.00/0.74/0.50. The factory propeller shaft is two-piece with rubber flex couplers at both ends and a center support bearing. The 226 mm differential bolts into the rear cradle on two front bushings and one rear bushing (rear bolt is torque-angle-yield, 162 ft-lb + 60\u00b0), with plug-in halfshafts retained by circlips.\n\nKey road-race decisions. (1) Clutch: factory twin-disc copes with 650 lb-ft on the street but a race car benefits from a lower-inertia twin/multi-disc pack and an adjustable hydraulic release bearing (Tilton 60-6241 / 60-6111 list TR6060 applications) with a remote bleeder. (2) Containment: fit an SFI 6.x steel bellhousing (or SFI blanket where permitted) and dial-indicate runout. (3) Gearbox: TR-6060 is durable when cooled and shifted cleanly; for endurance or a high-output custom engine consider a TREMEC Magnum XL (700 lb-ft) or a sequential dog box (Holinger RD6-S 880 N\u00b7m endurance rating, Xtrac as on the Camaro GT4.R) with paddle shift. (4) Driveshaft: replace the two-piece shaft and flex discs with a one-piece carbon or aluminum shaft (DSS GMCA19-C, QA1 JJ-22208) and add safety loops. (5) Differential: the eLSD is excellent but depends on GM network control; with a standalone race ECU most builders convert to a mechanical plate LSD and solid-mount the diff (BMR BK058/BK059/BK060). (6) Cooling: GM fits trans and diff coolers to the ZL1 1LE and warns of high axle temps on the first track day; race cars need ducted coolers with thermostats and temp logging.\n\nRules. Most sanctioning bodies (NASA, SCCA, Trans Am, etc.) have drivetrain safety requirements that vary by class: SFI-rated bellhousing or flywheel shield, driveshaft safety loop(s), catch cans for gearbox/axle breathers, lockwired drain plugs and sealed tunnel/shifter openings. Always check your class rulebook and tech inspector.\n\nFluids and intervals (GM). Manual transmission fluid change every 15 h of track use. Rear axle fluid needs 1,500 mi (2,400 km) break-in before track use; drain and refill after the first event then every 24 h of racing. ZL1 coupe with eLSD: 1.5 L axle fluid + 160 mL eLSD actuation fluid; LT1 non-eLSD axle 1.1 L. Clutch shares DOT 4 with the brakes.\n\nCommon failure points. Torn flex couplers and center bearing isolators, CV boot tears from exhaust heat, diff bushing deflection/wheel hop, overheated diff/gearbox oil, HRB seal failure from over-stroking, clutch contamination from input/rear-main seals, and loose AN fittings inside the bellhousing.",
  "buildNotes": [
    "Phase 3: with the rear cradle on the car (suspension team), install diff mount bushings/lockouts, raise the differential into the cradle and torque front bolts then the rear TAY bolt (new bolt).",
    "Phase 4: install halfshafts (seal protector, circlip pull-test) and torque axle nuts to GM SI with wheels loaded.",
    "Phase 5: engine on stand - pilot bearing, dial-indicate bellhousing runout (offset dowels if needed), flywheel (3-pass torque), clutch pack with alignment tool, measure finger height.",
    "Phase 6: set HRB height and plumb its internal hose/bleeder; mate gearbox (pre-filled) to engine; install crossmember/mount; fit driveshaft and safety loops; connect shift rod/shifter; set driveline angles.",
    "Phase 6: route -3 AN clutch line to the bulkhead; bleed via remote bleeder; set pedal over-travel stop.",
    "Phase 7: plumb trans and diff cooler circuits (pump below oil level, filter, thermostat, ducted cooler), vents to catch cans.",
    "Phase 8: wire sensors (trans temp, diff temp, gear position, clutch position, VSS, reverse lockout), pump control in PDM, eLSD (if retained) or delete strategy, sequential GCU if fitted.",
    "Phase 11: fill gearbox and axle (plus cooler volume), bleed coolers, check for leaks, break in axle per GM before hard track use, re-torque driveline fasteners after shakedown and paint-mark them."
  ],
